{"answerArea":{"calculator":false,"chi2Table":false,"periodicTable":false,"tTable":false,"zTable":false},"hints":[{"content":"First, we note that $q=-4$ makes the denominator zero and so cannot be a solution.\n\nNow, we multiply both sides of the equation by $2q+8$ to get rid of the quotient. ","images":{},"replace":false,"widgets":{}},{"content":"$\\begin{align}\n\\dfrac{10}{2q + 8} \u0026= \\dfrac{1}{10}\n\\\\\\\\\n(2q+8)\\cdot \\dfrac{10}{2q + 8} \u0026= (2q+8)\\cdot \\dfrac{1}{10}\n\\\\\\\\\n10\u0026=\\dfrac15 q+\\dfrac45\n\\\\\\\\\n\\dfrac{46}{5}\u0026=\\dfrac15 q\n\\\\\\\\\n46\u0026=q\n\\end{align}$","images":{},"replace":false,"widgets":{}},{"content":"We get that $q=46$ is a potential solution. It doesn't make the denominator zero, so it is indeed a solution.\n\n**In conclusion, $q=46$.**","images":{},"replace":false,"widgets":{}}],"itemDataVersion":{"major":0,"minor":1},"question":{"content":"**Solve for $q$.**\n\n$\\dfrac{10}{2q + 8} = \\dfrac{1}{10}$\n\n$q =$ [[☃ numeric-input 1]]","images":{},"widgets":{"numeric-input 1":{"alignment":"default","graded":true,"options":{"answers":[{"maxError":0,"message":"","simplify":"required","status":"correct","strict":false,"value":46}],"coefficient":false,"labelText":"","multipleNumberInput":false,"size":"normal","static":false},"static":false,"type":"numeric-input","version":{"major":0,"minor":0}}}}}
